1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 { The Italian game is one of the oldest openings in chess. It was developed in the 16th and 17th centuries by famous Italian masters Damiano, Polerio, and Greco. It has always been frequently seen in beginners' and amateurs' games but its popularity among strong players gradually ceased in favor of the Ruy Lopez, which was considered a better try for an advantage until very recently. However, things have changed dramatically in recent years. Due to the futility of their attempts against the Berlin Defence, top players have started seeking ways to pose Black problems in the long-forgotten Italian. As a result of the trend set by Carlsen, Anand, Caruana, Karjakin, Vachier- Lagrave, Giri and other strong players it has suddenly become more popular than the Ruy Lopez and its theory has grown enormously. Unlike just a few years ago, Black is nowadays required to know the nuances of different lines and move orders to obtain a good position out of the opening. } Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 { -KEY- } O-O { The position is very likely to transpose to one of the lines of the 4. O-O Nf6 5. d3 O-O 6. c3 move order. However, there are a few lines of independent value. } 6. Bb3 d5 7. Qe2 { This method of play has been frequently used by Tiviakov. White wants to develop an attack on the kingside by moves like h3, g4, Nbd2-f1-g3, Bg5 played in some order. It is objectively dubious, but Black must act energetically to prove it. } a5 $1 { The key move with a typical idea of 'softening up' the b3 bishop. } 8. a4 Be6 { With an unpleasant threat of ...dxe4. Somehow Sergei Tiviakov avoids mentioning this move on his ChessBase DVD 'The Bishop’s Opening and The Italian Game'. } 9. Bc2 h6 { If White castles short now or on the next moves, his concept fails and Black is simply better thanks to his superiority in the centre. However it is not easy for White to develop kingside play, as illustrated by the following variation: } 10. Nbd2 Re8 11. h3 Bb6 { A useful waiting move. } 12. Nf1 Nh7 $15 { White's plans on the kingside are halted by the ...Nf8-g6-f4 maneuver combined with play in the centre. } 
