1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 { The Italian game is one of the oldest openings in chess. It was developed in the 16th and 17th centuries by famous Italian masters Damiano, Polerio, and Greco. It has always been frequently seen in beginners' and amateurs' games but its popularity among strong players gradually ceased in favor of the Ruy Lopez, which was considered a better try for an advantage until very recently. However, things have changed dramatically in recent years. Due to the futility of their attempts against the Berlin Defence, top players have started seeking ways to pose Black problems in the long-forgotten Italian. As a result of the trend set by Carlsen, Anand, Caruana, Karjakin, Vachier- Lagrave, Giri and other strong players it has suddenly become more popular than the Ruy Lopez and its theory has grown enormously. Unlike just a few years ago, Black is nowadays required to know the nuances of different lines and move orders to obtain a good position out of the opening. } Bc5 4. Nc3 { By playing the knight to c3, White abandons the typical Italian idea of building up with c3-d4 and strives for a symmetrical position a tempo up. It's not a particularly threatening approach, but it's still worth knowing some nuances here. } d6 ( { As we will see, this is a more cunning option than going for total symmetry with } 4... Nf6 ) 
5. h3 { This stops ...Bg4 but one can hardly believe it's a challenging move. Still, it's seen very often in practice. -KEY- } Nf6 6. O-O O-O 7. d3 Na5 $1 { As usual, going after the bishop is best. } 8. Bg5 Nxc4 9. dxc4 Be6 { Preparing to trade the knight coming to d5. } 10. Nd5 Bxd5 11. cxd5 h6 12. Bxf6 Qxf6 $11 { Black has promising prospects of play along the f-file, e.g. ...g6, ...Qg7, ...f5 } 
